Thank you for adding better character level support, Jolie! Also, Claude was impressed by your testing. It said, "the code is sound and unusually well-tested". Great work!

@katherinejensen00 reviewed 15 files and all commit messages, and made 9 comments.
Reviewable status: all files reviewed, 8 unresolved discussions (waiting on jolierabideau).


extensions/src/platform-scripture-editor/src/character-marker-menu.utils.ts line 102 at r1 (raw file):

      : undefined;

  const markerMenuItems: MarkerMenuItem[] = [];

[Low] Simplify the item build and the sort.
The const markerMenuItems = [] + Object.values(...).forEach(push(...)) is a flatMap: Object.values(markerDetails.children).flatMap(markers => markers.filter(isCharacterMarker).map((marker): MarkerMenuItem => …)). And at line 130 the comparator's ?? a.title is dead — every generated item has a marker and the remove row is prepended after sorting — so a.marker.localeCompare(b.marker) is both simpler and truer to the invariant.

extensions/src/platform-scripture-editor/src/character-marker-menu.utils.ts line 123 at r1 (raw file):

              if (currentCharacterMarker && changeCharacterMarker)
                changeCharacterMarker(currentCharacterMarker, marker);
              else editorRef.current?.insertMarker(marker);

[Medium] Please verify that "add instead of change" can't nest character markers.
When a marker is applied but changeCharacterMarker isn't wired, picking a different marker falls through to insertMarker, inserting a second character marker over an existing span. It's unreachable today (nothing passes currentCharacterMarker), and I couldn't inspect @eten-tech-foundation/platform-editor to see whether insertMarker nests, replaces, or rejects here. Worth confirming against the editor before PT-XXX-D passes a real currentCharacterMarker, since nested/overlapping character markers are invalid in some USFM consumers.

extensions/src/platform-scripture-editor/src/localized-strings.test.ts line 15 at r1 (raw file):

  // JSON.parse returns `any`; asserting the known shape of localized strings contribution files
  // eslint-disable-next-line no-type-assertion/no-type-assertion
  const stringsFile = JSON.parse(readFileSync(stringsFilePath, 'utf-8')) as LocalizedStringsFile;

[Low] You can drop this eslint-disable entirely.
JSON.parse returns any, which assigns to a typed target without a cast, so const stringsFile: LocalizedStringsFile = JSON.parse(readFileSync(...), 'utf-8') satisfies the type with no as and no suppression — which is what the eslint-disable-discipline rule prefers.

extensions/src/platform-scripture-editor/src/localized-strings.test.ts line 43 at r1 (raw file):

      const es = localizedStrings.es[key];
      expect(es.charAt(0)).toMatch(/[A-ZÁÉÍÓÚÜÑ]/);
      expect(es.slice(1)).toBe(es.slice(1).toLowerCase());

[Low] This sentence-case check will reject correct future translations and can throw instead of failing cleanly.
es.slice(1) === es.slice(1).toLowerCase() forbids any non-initial capital, so a future key whose Spanish legitimately contains a proper noun or an acronym (e.g. USFM) fails a correct translation. Related: charAt(0) won't match a Spanish opening ¿/¡; a missing es value makes es.charAt(0) throw a TypeError rather than report the missing string; and it uses toLowerCase not toLocaleLowerCase. It's copied from legacy-comment-manager, so this is really a shared-pattern gap — worth loosening (or extracting) before more keys ride on it.

extensions/src/platform-scripture-editor/src/character-marker-menu.utils.ts line 137 at r1 (raw file):

  // menu's search box is empty: the row has no marker code to match on, so a search can only match
  // its title, and `MarkerMenu` renders title matches after every code match.
  if (!currentCharacterMarker || !removeCharacterMarker) return sortedMarkerMenuItems;

**[Medium] The menu can return a "remove only" list, which contradicts the documented @returns.
The @returns says the result is [] when the parent "contributes no character markers." But for the 14 parents whose children are all non-character (mt, qs, cp, h, h1–h3, ide, b, mte1, ms3, mt1–mt3), the childless early-return is bypassed, the filter yields [], and if a marker is applied with removeCharacterMarker supplied you get [removeRow] — a menu that offers only "Remove" and nothing to add. It's latent (needs the remove op), but before PT-XXX-D either return [] when there are no character children, or update the doc to describe the remove-only case.

extensions/src/platform-scripture-editor/src/platform-scripture-editor.utils.ts line 34 at r1 (raw file):

import { ScriptureRange } from 'platform-scripture-editor';
import type { SharedProjectsInfo } from 'platform-scripture';
import { MutableRefObject } from 'react';

[Low] One-line hardening for the import boundary: make these import type.
MutableRefObject, EditorRef, and MarkerMenuItem are bare value imports used only in type positions — safe today only because the compiler elides them. Switching to import type { … } makes the erasure structural: a future value-use becomes a compile error at the import line instead of a silent runtime require('react'). Given this is the file whose header warns about exactly this, it's a fitting place to practice it.

